      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name likely comes from Switzerland’s Mariastein Abbey after Father Brunner donated a painted Madonna of Mariastein.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Stein%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1833, three German immigrants founded the settlement that would become Maria Stein, later merging with a nearby community across 0.5 miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.marioncommunities.com/maria-stein.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Maria Stein Convent and its large church host the Shrine of the Holy Relics, a centerpiece of the town’s religious heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Stein%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area is part of the Land of the Cross‑Tipped Churches, where German Catholic missionaries built churches with steeples topped by crosses.",
